course: dictionary
lesson: 7629
title: Words on the move
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A procession is a line of people, cars or animals moving slowly forward in an orderly way, often as part of a ceremony or celebration. Look at the clues: «a grand procession marches in» tells you it is big, slow and organised.
explain: The word can also mean a long series of things that come one after another, as in a procession of visitors. Here the clue is «one after another», not walking along a road.
explain: Ask yourself: what is moving, and how? In Alice's story, soldiers, courtiers, royal children and guests all walk in order, so procession must mean a line moving forward together. To check, swap in "a line of" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A procession is a line of people, cars or animals moving slowly forward in an orderly way.
fact: A procession can also be a long series of things that come one after another.
fact: Useful phrases: «in procession», «a funeral procession», «a torchlight procession», «lead the procession».
fact: «Procession» is a noun, and its plural is «processions».
fact: In Alice's story, the procession has soldiers, courtiers, royal children, guests, the Knave of Hearts and, last of all, the King and Queen of Hearts.
exercise: In Alice's story, who carries the King's crown on a cushion in the procession? | The gardeners | Alice | The White Rabbit | *The Knave of Hearts
exercise: What does procession mean? | A loud noise at the end of a game | A kind of hat worn at weddings | A small, quiet room for reading | *A line of people or things moving slowly forward in order
exercise: Which phrase means to be at the front of a procession? | *lead the procession | count the procession | hide the procession | miss the procession
test: In «a torchlight procession», what do the people most likely carry? | *Lit torches | Shopping bags | Balloons | Umbrellas
test: In «a procession of visitors», what does procession mean? | A type of cake | *A long series of visitors coming one after another | A small quiet room | A shout of surprise
exam: The children walked two by two, in ___, behind the band. | *procession | puddle | pillow | parcel
